+
+**Evidence 2 — the drift is cumulative, and the stranded value is unreachable.** Two
+10,000 USDT deposits, then two half-position withdrawals (deposit fee set to 0, so the
+only moving part is the withdrawal fee):
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/audit/FeeDriftRegression.t.sol' -vv
+Ran 2 tests for test/audit/FeeDriftRegression.t.sol:FeeDriftRegressionTest
+[FAIL: aUSDT held must equal assets accounted for: 30000000 != 0] test_DriftGrowsWithEachWithdrawal() (gas: 373323)
+Logs:
+  drift after deposits         : 0
+  drift after 1st withdrawal   : 15000000
+  drift after 2nd withdrawal   : 30000000
+
+[FAIL: vault holds aUSDT that no shareholder can ever claim: 30000000 != 0] test_StrandedFeesAreUnrecoverableAfterFullExit() (gas: 259482)
+Logs:
+  shares outstanding           : 0
+  assets accounted for         : 0
+  aUSDT still held by the vault: 30000000
+
+Suite result: FAILED. 0 passed; 2 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 1.35ms (772.59µs CPU time)
+```
+
+Zero drift before any withdrawal, 15 USDT after one, 30 USDT after two — it accumulates
+without bound, 0.3% of every withdrawal ever made. The second test shows the end state:
+shares outstanding 0, assets accounted for 0, and 30 USDT of aUSDT in the vault that no
+`withdraw()` call can reach (it needs shares) and that no sweep or rescue function
+exists to recover.
+
+**Fix:** decide what the fee is *for* and make the code say it.
+* If it accrues to remaining holders (what the NatSpec claims): subtract only
+  `netAssets` from `totalAssetsStored`, so the fee stays in both custody and accounting
+  and genuinely lifts the share price.
+* If it is protocol revenue: withdraw `grossAssets` from Aave and transfer `fee` to a
+  treasury address, keeping `totalAssetsStored -= grossAssets`.
+
+Either way, adopting the F2 fix (`totalAssets()` derived from `aUsdt.balanceOf`) makes
+this class of bug unrepresentable, and keep the equality invariant in CI.
+
+---
+
+## F4 — HIGH: `setDepositFee` is unbounded; the owner can confiscate deposits or brick the vault.
+
+`setDepositFee` accepts any `uint256`. The shipped suite only ever tries 0, 10 and 25.
+Fuzzing the full accepted domain immediately leaves the working range.
+
+**Evidence:**
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/audit/DepositFeeFuzz.t.sol' -vv
+Ran 4 tests for test/audit/DepositFeeFuzz.t.sol:DepositFeeFuzzTest
+[FAIL: panic: arithmetic underflow or overflow (0x11); counterexample: calldata=0x483eb8c800000000000000000000000000000000000000037a1ceb43007540df60753952 args=[275476619656034651987527416146 [2.754e29]]] testFuzz_AnyAcceptedFeeKeepsDepositUsable(uint256) (runs: 0, μ: 0, ~: 0)
+[PASS] test_Boundary_10000Bps_BricksDeposits() (gas: 30577)
+[PASS] test_Boundary_10001Bps_UnderflowPanic() (gas: 28581)
+[FAIL: owner must not be able to seize alice's deposit: 9091000000 >= 2000000] test_Boundary_9999Bps_ConfiscatesDepositToIncumbentHolders() (gas: 393560)
+Logs:
+  alice deposited        : 100000000000
+  alice redeemable value : 90910000000
+  owner redeemable value : 9091000000
+
+Suite result: FAILED. 2 passed; 2 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 1.80ms (1.90ms CPU time)
+```
+
+The three boundary values fail in three different ways, which is why they are separate
+tests (the two marked PASS are *demonstrations*: they assert that the revert happens):
+
+| fee | outcome |
+|---|---|
+| **9,999 bps** — nearest valid value below the limit | No revert. 99.99% of the deposit is silently handed to incumbent shareholders. In the test the owner front-runs Alice's 100,000 USDT deposit from a **1 USDT** position and walks away with a **9,091 USDT** claim — a 9,090× gain taken directly out of Alice. Alice's own claim drops to 90,910. |
+| **10,000 bps** — the exact limit | `netAmount == 0` → `shares == 0` → `NoSharesMinted`. Deposits permanently bricked. |
+| **10,001 bps** — first value beyond | `amount - fee` underflows → `Panic 0x11`. A different code path, and a worse revert (no named error, so any integrating UI shows an unexplained failure). |
+
+The fuzzer needed zero runs to find a 2.75e29 bps fee. Note this is a *live* power, not
+just a deploy-time misconfiguration: it can be exercised at any moment, including in the
+same block as a victim's pending deposit.
+
+**Fix:** add `uint256 public constant MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS = 100;` (1%, or whatever the
+product actually needs) and `if (newFeeBps > MAX_DEPOSIT_FEE_BPS) revert FeeTooHigh();`
+in `setDepositFee`. Put the setter behind a timelock so depositors can exit ahead of any
+increase, and keep the domain fuzz test in CI so the bound cannot silently regress.
+
+---
+
+## F5 — MEDIUM: `pause()` freezes existing depositors with no exit, and can be made permanent.
+
+`withdraw()` carries `whenNotPaused`. The shipped suite checks that pause blocks
+*deposits* (`test_PauseBlocksDeposit`) and never asks what pause does to someone already
+in. The answer is that their funds are frozen at the owner's discretion, indefinitely,
+with no `emergencyWithdraw` or redeem-while-paused path. Combined with `renounceOwnership`
+(inherited from `Ownable` and left unguarded), the owner — or anyone who steals the owner
+key — can pause and then renounce, leaving every deposit locked forever.
+
+**Evidence** (these two PASS: the assertion is that the bad outcome *is* reachable):
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path 'test/audit/OwnerPowers.t.sol' -vv
+Ran 2 tests for test/audit/OwnerPowers.t.sol:OwnerPowersTest
+[PASS] test_OwnerCanRenounceLeavingVaultPermanentlyPaused() (gas: 201032)
+[PASS] test_PauseFreezesExistingDepositorFundsWithNoExit() (gas: 198965)
+Suite result: ok. 2 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 843.63µs (315.58µs CPU time)
+```
+
+`test_PauseFreezesExistingDepositorFundsWithNoExit` deposits 10,000 USDT, pauses, and
+asserts `withdraw` reverts with `EnforcedPause` while the position is still worth 10,000.
+`test_OwnerCanRenounceLeavingVaultPermanentlyPaused` pauses, renounces, and asserts the
+owner is `address(0)`, `unpause()` reverts for everyone, and `paused()` is still true.
+
+**Fix:** drop `whenNotPaused` from `withdraw()` — an emergency brake should stop new
+risk entering, not trap the people already exposed. Override `renounceOwnership()` to
+revert. Put ownership behind a multisig, and a timelock for anything that can move value.
+
+---
+
+## Residual risk — what I did *not* establish
+
+* Findings F2 and F3 were reached with the F1 `approve` bug neutralised at the test
+  boundary. Once F1 is fixed for real, re-run the fork suite unmodified to confirm they
+  reproduce through the genuine code path.
+* The fork is pinned at block 21,000,000. USDT is currently not fee-on-transfer, but its
+  owner can enable a transfer fee; if that happened, `safeTransferFrom` would deliver
+  less than `amount` while `totalAssetsStored += amount` books the full figure — another
+  accounting/custody break. Untested here because it is not currently reachable on chain.
+* Aave-side conditions (supply caps, a frozen or paused USDT reserve, aUSDT illiquidity)
+  are not covered; `deposit`/`withdraw` have no handling for a reverting or partial
+  Aave response, and `aavePool.withdraw`'s return value is discarded.
+* The rounding direction of `convertToShares`/`convertToAssets` (both round down via
+  `mulDiv`) was not probed for a donation/first-depositor inflation attack. The usual
+  vector is blunted because `totalAssets()` is an internal counter rather than a balance
+  — but that stops being true the moment you apply the F2 fix, so re-examine it then.
+* `slither` is clean at high/medium (output above), so it should not be read as
+  corroborating anything; it did not find any of the five.
+
+---
+
+## Reproducing all of this
+
+Nothing under `src/`, `test/UsdtYieldVault.t.sol` or `test/mocks/` was modified. New
+files, all under `test/audit/`:
+
+| file | what it searches |
+|---|---|
+| `DepositFeeFuzz.t.sol` | domain fuzz + the three fee boundaries (F4) |
+| `VaultHandler.sol` / `SolvencyInvariant.t.sol` | handler-driven accounting-vs-custody invariant (F3) |
+| `FeeDriftRegression.t.sol` | deterministic proof the drift accumulates and is unrecoverable (F3) |
+| `MainnetFork.t.sol` | pinned fork, real USDT + real Aave V3 (F1, F2) |
+| `OwnerPowers.t.sol` | pause/renounce reachability (F5) |
+
+```bash
+forge test --match-path 'test/audit/DepositFeeFuzz.t.sol' -vv
+forge test --match-path 'test/audit/SolvencyInvariant.t.sol'
+forge test --match-path 'test/audit/FeeDriftRegression.t.sol' -vv
+forge test --match-path 'test/audit/OwnerPowers.t.sol' -vv
+
+# fork tests need an ARCHIVE endpoint that serves block 21,000,000.
+# With no env var set they default to https://eth.drpc.org, which was confirmed to
+# answer a historical eth_call at that block:
+#   cast call 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7 "totalSupply()(uint256)" \
+#     --block 21000000 --rpc-url https://eth.drpc.org
+#   -> 54965840345993792
+export MAINNET_RPC_URL=<your archive endpoint>   # optional
+forge test --match-path 'test/audit/MainnetFork.t.sol' -vv
+forge test --match-test test_DepositRevertsAgainstRealUsdt -vvvv
+```
